ction.","keywords":["continuous-passive"],"skill":"continuous-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-49","fingerprint":"bbfd9ad8f6fc6d3550481ddfb1dc1f90119c2d84d9c7c021b12bb5f3ffe2280e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Ongoing passive = **be + being + past participle**.","After the finite form of be, **being prepared** marks an ongoing passive action.","Choose **being prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This makes simple passive, not the requested continuous passive.","B":"The passive part needs a past participle after being.","C":"After the finite form of be, **being prepared** marks an ongoing passive action.","D":"Been belongs after perfect have, not directly after is."}}},{"id":"32d9382e-3e4f-5292-a461-504e6098d03c","topicSlug":"passive-voice","level":"B1","prompt":"Use a modal passive: “The evening meal must ____ before Friday.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"is prepared"},{"id":"B","text":"be preparing"},{"id":"C","text":"be prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"been prepared"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"The object receives the required action: **must be + participle**.","explanation":"After must, use base **be** and participle **prepared**.","keywords":["modal-passive"],"skill":"modal-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-49","fingerprint":"59bebd5896b2f4915cfadfd56206facedd27d02eb2e183138d967ea077b17231","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The object receives the required action: **must be + participle**.","After must, use base **be** and participle **prepared**.","Choose **be prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A finite is cannot follow must.","B":"This is active continuous, not passive.","C":"After must, use base **be** and participle **prepared**.","D":"Must needs have before been; this task asks for a simple modal passive."}}},{"id":"9489fa69-02c2-585b-a6e4-354662e4c9d6","topicSlug":"passive-voice","level":"B1","prompt":"Use present simple passive: “The rowing boat ____ every week.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"is repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"are repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"is to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"is repaired"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The task asks for **receiver + present be + past participle**.","explanation":"The singular/uncountable subject takes **is**, followed by participle **repaired**.","keywords":["present-passive"],"skill":"present-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-50","fingerprint":"3d1f9d8f756395abd5408640af36208fd6f5d881e10f7a8054f254825770486f","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The task asks for **receiver + present be + past participle**.","The singular/uncountable subject takes **is**, followed by participle **repaired**.","Choose **is repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is active continuous, not passive.","B":"This form of be does not agree with the subject.","C":"This is not the present-simple passive construction.","D":"The singular/uncountable subject takes **is**, followed by participle **repaired**."}}},{"id":"2733d268-849e-506a-abab-5d1525f240fe","topicSlug":"passive-voice","level":"B1","prompt":"Use past simple passive: “The rowing boat ____ yesterday.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"did repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"was repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"were repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"was repairing"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The event is past and the subject receives the action.","explanation":"Past simple passive with this subject is **was repaired**.","keywords":["past-passive"],"skill":"past-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-50","fingerprint":"95f7eb0e8ba9baf2ebe32d15c3fd314f3acd55e6674b50915b811ffb5815c5e8","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The event is past and the subject receives the action.","Past simple passive with this subject is **was repaired**.","Choose **was repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Did cannot replace passive be before a participle.","B":"Past simple passive with this subject is **was repaired**.","C":"This form of be does not agree with the subject in this factual clause.","D":"This is active past continuous, not passive."}}},{"id":"882207ba-561d-54b3-a074-ef002547f520","topicSlug":"passive-voice","level":"B1","prompt":"Use present continuous passive: “The rowing boat is ____ right now.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"been repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"being repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"being repaired"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Ongoing passive = **be + being + past participle**.","explanation":"After the finite form of be, **being repaired** marks an ongoing passive action.","keywords":["continuous-passive"],"skill":"continuous-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-50","fingerprint":"8bf30c669db434877eae8fbc3229f59848575423a5df41fb929a8d974feec589","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Ongoing passive = **be + being + past participle**.","After the finite form of be, **being repaired** marks an ongoing passive action.","Choose **being repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Been belongs after perfect have, not directly after is.","B":"This makes simple passive, not the requested continuous passive.","C":"The passive part needs a past participle after being.","D":"After the finite form of be, **being repaired** marks an ongoing passive action."}}},{"id":"85021a71-de3d-554f-aee8-aabdbae9ac8f","topicSlug":"passive-voice","level":"B1","prompt":"Use a modal passive: “The rowing boat must ____ before Friday.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"be repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"be repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"been repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"is repaired"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The object receives the required action: **must be + participle**.","explanation":"After must, use base **be** and participle **repaired**.","keywords":["modal-passive"],"skill":"modal-passive","contextKey":"passive-voice-50","fingerprint":"2bca7f55632096c59f47c117cb32c4169d9c5bd8617197a9c49f562d5c238946","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-passives"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The object receives the required action: **must be + participle**.","After must, use base **be** and participle **repaired**.","Choose **be repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is active continuous, not passive.","B":"After must, use base **be** and participle **repaired**.","C":"Must needs have before been; this task asks for a simple modal passive.","D":"A finite is cannot follow must."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Change the focus of a clause while keeping its tense and meaning clear.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Change the starting point","explanation":"A passive clause makes the receiver or affected participant the subject. Build it with **be + past participle**. A by phrase can name the agent when that information matters, but it is often omitted when unknown or obvious.","pattern":"The report **was checked** by the editor.","commonMistakes":["An intransitive verb such as arrive normally has no object to turn into a passive subject."],"examples":["Use present simple passive: “The visitor guide **is prepared** every week.”","Use present simple passive: “The old portrait **is restored** every week.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Keep the original tense","explanation":"The form of **be** carries tense and agreement. The past participle stays the same. Present simple passive uses am/is/are; past simple passive uses was/were. Find the new subject’s number before choosing be.","pattern":"The letters **are delivered**. / The letter **was delivered**.","commonMistakes":["A past participle is not a past-tense marker by itself: is delivered is present passive."],"examples":["Use past simple passive: “The visitor guide **was prepared** yesterday.”","Use past simple passive: “The old portrait **was restored** yesterday.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Continuous and perfect passives","explanation":"A continuous passive adds being before the participle. A perfect passive adds been after have. Read the chain from left to right: first tense, then aspect, then passive.","pattern":"It **is being repaired**. / It **has been repaired**.","commonMistakes":["Being and been are not interchangeable: continuous uses being; perfect uses been."],"examples":["Use present continuous passive: “The visitor guide is **being prepared** right now.”","Use present continuous passive: “The old portrait is **being restored** right now.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Negatives, questions and modals","explanation":"Put not after the first auxiliary and move that auxiliary before the subject in questions. A modal passive uses modal + be + participle. Do not add do to a passive that already contains be.","pattern":"**Was** it repaired? / It **must be repaired**.","commonMistakes":["A passive is a choice of focus, not automatically better or more formal than active voice."],"examples":["Use a modal passive: “The visitor guide must **be prepared** before Friday.”","Use a modal passive: “The old portrait must **be restored** before Friday.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["The report **was checked** by the editor.","The letters **are delivered**. / The letter **was delivered**.","It **is being repaired**. / It **has been repaired**.","**Was** it repaired? / It **must be repaired**."]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["degree-comparison"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
